.TH CCOL_MEMPOOL_CREATE 3 "2026-09-17" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_mempool_create \- create a fixed-size memory pool
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cmempool.h>

ccol_mempool *ccol_mempool_create(size_t elem_count,
                        size_t elem_size,
                        bool fallback_to_dynamic_memory,
                        bool single_threaded,
                        ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mmgmt_procs,
                        char **err);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a memory pool that manages a fixed number of fixed-size elements.
All allocations return elements of the same size. Provides O(1) allocation
and deallocation using a free list.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
elem_count                 Number of elements in the pool (must be > 0)
elem_size                  Size of each element in bytes (must be > 0,
                          minimum sizeof(uintptr_t), maximum SIZE_MAX
                          minus the stride rounding)
fallback_to_dynamic_memory If true, allocate from heap when pool exhausted
single_threaded           If true, omit locking (faster but not thread-safe)
mmgmt_procs               Custom memory management, or NULL for default
err                       Optional pointer to receive error string
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns pointer to newly created memory pool on success
Returns NULL on failure
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The stride between elements is elem_size rounded up to 16 bytes and then,
unless the library was built with -DCCOL_MEMPOOL_COMPACT_LAYOUT=1, on up
to a power of two; see "Compile-Time Configuration" in README.md. Either
way every element is aligned for any object type, not just the first.
This matches what malloc() guarantees, so any object that can be stored
in heap memory can be stored in an entry
If elem_size < sizeof(uintptr_t), it is rounded up
Returns NULL with error if elem_size would overflow size_t once the
stride rounding is applied, or if elem_count and that stride together
would overflow it: a pool holds one stride per element followed by one
status byte per element, all in one block
With fallback enabled, pool never fails allocation (until system OOM)
Pool must be destroyed with ccol_mempool_destroy() when done

Sizing for performance:

Give a thread-safe pool at least 8 elements. Below that there is no room
to carve out a per-thread cache, so every allocation and every free takes
the pool's lock and concurrent callers serialise against one another.
8 is a floor, not a target: cache size grows with the pool, so a pool
sized for real use does considerably better than one sized at exactly 8.

A pool that receives a cache allocates a reserve beyond elem_count. The
reserve is what keeps elem_count obtainable by any thread no matter how
many entries other threads are holding in their own caches. It costs
memory, at most half of elem_count, falling to a quarter at 8192
elements, an eighth at 16384, and to a few percent or less from roughly
65536 upward; the reserve stops growing once a cache reaches its maximum
depth, so a large pool pays proportionally far less than a small one.

ccol_mempool_total_capacity() reports elem_count, and
ccol_mempool_used_count() counts only entries genuinely handed out rather
than entries merely pulled into a cache. What the reserve does change is
how exact the upper end of that count is under concurrent load: an entry
leaves a thread's cache with no lock held and so with no count to
consult, so entries are committed to a cache before it is known whether
they will be needed. That decision favours the guarantee above, and in
exchange concurrent callers can briefly hold a few entries more than
elem_count at once, never more than elem_count plus the reserve. The
memory involved is allocated when the pool is created either way, so
nothing grows at run time. See ccol_mempool_total_capacity(3).

Pools created with single_threaded = true, and pools created by
ccol_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(), never use the cache.
Neither the reserve nor the 8-element floor applies to them.

The right elem_count, elem_size and single_threaded setting depend on the
access pattern, the number of threads and the object lifetime of the
program using the pool, and no single choice is best for all of them.
Measure rather than assume: "make bench" in the source tree runs the
pool's own benchmarks alongside the system allocator on the current
machine.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
Thread-safe when single_threaded is false, using a mutex.

On the common path a thread-safe pool takes no lock at all. Each thread
keeps a small private cache of entries and reaches the shared free list
only to refill or flush it, so threads allocating and freeing
concurrently do not contend with one another, so throughput rises as
threads are added.

Entries held in one thread's cache return to the shared free list when
that thread exits. Destroying the pool releases its whole block, those
entries included; the cache's own allocation is a separate matter, and
ccol_mempool_destroy(3) gives the points at which it goes back to your
allocator. Join a thread before destroying a pool it used, which is
sufficient because a thread's cleanup runs before pthread_join()
returns.

Passing single_threaded = true removes all locking and all caching. Such
a pool must never be used from more than one thread.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
